---
import TextbookCallout from '../TextbookCallout.astro';
import TextbookChunk from '../TextbookChunk.astro';
import TextbookPageIntro from '../TextbookPageIntro.astro';
import TextbookSectionBlock from '../TextbookSectionBlock.astro';
import type { TextbookChapterModel, TextbookSectionModel } from '../../../lib/textbook-model';

interface Props {
  chapter: TextbookChapterModel;
}

const { chapter } = Astro.props as Props;

const requireSection = (code: string): TextbookSectionModel => {
  const section = chapter.sections.find((candidate) => candidate.code === code);
  if (!section) {
    throw new Error(`Missing textbook section "${code}" for rebuilt chapter ${chapter.code}.`);
  }
  return section;
};

const b21 = requireSection('B2.1');
const b22 = requireSection('B2.2');
const b23 = requireSection('B2.3');
const b24 = requireSection('B2.4');
const b25 = requireSection('B2.5');
---

<TextbookPageIntro code={chapter.code} title={chapter.title} summary={chapter.summary} visibility={chapter.visibility}>
  <p>
    B2 turns computational thinking into working programs. The chapter moves from variables and
    control structures into data structures, algorithm efficiency, and persistent storage, so the
    strongest understanding comes from seeing how those ideas connect rather than treating each
    programming skill as isolated.
  </p>
  <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Reading pattern">
    <p>
      Build code from the inside out: store data correctly, control program flow clearly, then
      choose the right structure or algorithm for scale. Higher level students should then follow
      the recursion extension as a specific extra layer inside B2.4 rather than as a separate topic.
    </p>
  </TextbookCallout>
</TextbookPageIntro>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={b21}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="B2.1.1, B2.1.2"
    summary="Construct and trace programs that use suitable data types, distinguish between global and local scope, and extract substrings accurately."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Programming fundamentals begin with controlled storage. A variable stores a value that may
      change while the program runs, and the chosen data type affects what operations are valid. At
      this level, you should be able to trace where a value is stored, how it changes, and whether
      the variable belongs to the whole program or only to one function.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-variables-scope-and-substrings">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Concept</th>
          <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical example</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Data type</td>
          <td>The form of data a variable stores</td>
          <td>Integer, real number, string, Boolean</td>
          <td>Determines valid operations and storage behaviour</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Global variable</td>
          <td>A variable declared outside functions and available more widely</td>
          <td>A shared setting such as a tax rate or school name</td>
          <td>Useful for shared values, but can make tracing harder if overused</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Local variable</td>
          <td>A variable declared inside a function or block</td>
          <td>A temporary total or loop counter</td>
          <td>Keeps work contained and reduces unintended side effects</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Substring</td>
          <td>A smaller string taken from part of a longer string</td>
          <td>Extracting <code>Ste</code> from <code>Steven</code></td>
          <td>Supports validation, formatting, and data cleaning</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-tracing-scope-and-substring-use">Worked example: tracing scope and substring use</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-python">school_name = "St Georges"
student_count = 540

def make_label(student_name):
    year_group = "IB"
    code = student_name[0:3].upper()
    return year_group + "-" + code</code></pre>
    <p>
      In this example, <code>school_name</code> and <code>student_count</code> are global variables.
      Inside the function, <code>year_group</code> and <code>code</code> are local variables because
      they exist only while <code>make_label</code> runs. The expression
      <code>student_name[0:3]</code> extracts a substring and converts it into a short code.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B2.1.3, B2.1.4"
    summary="Use exception handling to keep a program under control when errors occur, and apply debugging techniques that expose faulty logic as well as faulty syntax."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      A robust program does more than run when everything is ideal. It also deals with unexpected
      inputs or missing resources without crashing immediately. Exception handling and debugging are
      linked because both aim to make program behaviour visible and manageable.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-exception-handling-and-debugging">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Technique</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Try and except or catch</td>
          <td>Intercept a runtime problem and provide an alternative response</td>
          <td>Prevent a crash when a string cannot be converted to an integer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Input validation</td>
          <td>Check data before processing it</td>
          <td>Reject a blank username before saving it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Trace table</td>
          <td>Record variable values step by step</td>
          <td>Check whether a loop updates values correctly</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Print or log statements</td>
          <td>Reveal intermediate state during execution</td>
          <td>Show the value of a counter before and after an update</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Breakpoint debugging</td>
          <td>Pause execution and inspect state in an IDE</td>
          <td>Check why a branch was taken at a decision point</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <pre><code class="language-python">try:
    age = int(input_value)
except ValueError:
    print("Enter a whole number")</code></pre>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        Debugging is not only for syntax errors. Many exam questions are really about logic errors,
        where the code runs but gives the wrong result because the sequence, condition, or update is
        incorrect.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={b22}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="B2.2.1, B2.2.2"
    summary="Compare fixed and flexible linear structures, then construct programs that store, access, and update data in arrays or lists."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Data structures organize values so that a program can access them efficiently and predictably.
      The syllabus keeps the focus on linear structures: fixed-size storage, dynamic storage, and the
      practical operations students need in programs.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-static-and-dynamic-structures">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Structure type</th>
          <th scope="col">Main feature</th>
          <th scope="col">Strength</th>
          <th scope="col">Limitation</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Static array</td>
          <td>Size is fixed once created</td>
          <td>Predictable indexing and memory use</td>
          <td>Cannot grow without creating new storage</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dynamic list</td>
          <td>Can grow or shrink during execution</td>
          <td>Flexible when the final size is unknown</td>
          <td>May require more overhead for resizing or insertion</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Different programming languages implement these structures in different ways, but the IB
      distinction is stable: static means fixed length, while dynamic means the structure can change
      size while the program runs.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-example-updating-a-linear-structure">Worked example: updating a linear structure</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-python">scores = [7, 6, 8]
scores.append(9)
scores[1] = 7</code></pre>
    <p>
      This short example uses a dynamic list. A new value is added with <code>append</code>, then an
      existing value is replaced through indexing. Those two actions already show why lists are useful
      when the program needs both storage and later modification.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B2.2.3, B2.2.4"
    summary="Explain stacks and queues by linking their ordering rule to the operations they allow and the problems they solve."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Stacks and queues are still linear structures, but they restrict where elements can be added or
      removed. That rule is what gives each structure its characteristic behaviour.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-stacks-and-queues">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Structure</th>
          <th scope="col">Ordering rule</th>
          <th scope="col">Core operations</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Stack</td>
          <td>LIFO: last in, first out</td>
          <td>Push, pop, peek</td>
          <td>Undo systems, function calls, recursion support</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Queue</td>
          <td>FIFO: first in, first out</td>
          <td>Enqueue, dequeue, front</td>
          <td>Printer jobs, customer lines, scheduling</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-trace-stack-vs-queue-behaviour">Worked trace: stack vs queue behaviour</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Operation</th>
          <th scope="col">Stack state</th>
          <th scope="col">Queue state</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Start</td>
          <td>Empty</td>
          <td>Empty</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Add A, then B, then C</td>
          <td>A, B, C on top</td>
          <td>A at front, then B, then C</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Remove one item</td>
          <td>C removed first</td>
          <td>A removed first</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Connection across the chapter">
      <p>
        The recursion section in B2.4 depends on the stack idea. Each new recursive call adds a new
        frame, and those frames are resolved in LIFO order when the function returns.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={b23}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="B2.3.1, B2.3.2, B2.3.3"
    summary="Construct programs that sequence instructions correctly, use selection for decisions, and apply loops for repeated work."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Programming constructs are the control tools that shape execution. A correct program does not
      only contain the right operations. It also places them in the right order, branches when a
      condition demands it, and repeats actions without unnecessary duplication.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-sequence-selection-and-loops">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Construct</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical question it answers</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Sequence</td>
          <td>Run instructions in the required order</td>
          <td>What must happen first, next, and last?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Selection</td>
          <td>Choose a path based on a condition</td>
          <td>What happens if the condition is true or false?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Loop</td>
          <td>Repeat a block while a condition holds or for a known number of times</td>
          <td>Which step needs to happen again?</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-combining-three-core-constructs">Worked example: combining three core constructs</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-python">total = 0

for mark in marks:
    total = total + mark

average = total / len(marks)

if average &gt;= 7:
    outcome = "higher level ready"
else:
    outcome = "needs more practice"</code></pre>
    <p>
      The loop calculates the total, the sequence places average calculation after the loop, and the
      selection structure assigns an outcome from the average. If the order changed, the program would
      fail or produce meaningless values.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B2.3.4"
    summary="Construct functions and use modularization so that a program is easier to read, test, debug, and reuse."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Modularization means dividing a larger solution into smaller named units with a clear purpose.
      In practical terms, that usually means writing functions that receive inputs, perform one
      focused task, and then return a result.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-functions-and-modularization">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Feature</th>
          <th scope="col">Role in a program</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Function name</td>
          <td>States the task clearly enough that the call is readable</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Parameter</td>
          <td>Accepts data needed by the function</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Return value</td>
          <td>Sends the result back to the calling code</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Local scope</td>
          <td>Keeps temporary work inside the function</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <pre><code class="language-python">def calculate_average(values):
    total = sum(values)
    return total / len(values)</code></pre>

    <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Evaluation rule">
      <p>
        Modularization is not about making as many functions as possible. It is about choosing useful
        boundaries so each function has a clear responsibility and the whole program is easier to
        follow.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={b24}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="B2.4.1"
    summary="Describe algorithm efficiency with Big O notation by focusing on how performance changes as input size grows."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Big O notation is a way of describing scalability. It does not give an exact running time in
      seconds. Instead, it shows how quickly the cost of an algorithm grows as the amount of data
      increases.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-big-o-and-scalability">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Complexity</th>
          <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical B2 example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>O(1)</code></td>
          <td>Cost stays constant as input size changes</td>
          <td>Accessing one known array position</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>O(log n)</code></td>
          <td>Cost grows slowly as the search space is repeatedly halved</td>
          <td>Binary search on sorted data</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>O(n)</code></td>
          <td>Cost grows in direct proportion to the input size</td>
          <td>Linear search in the worst case</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>O(n^2)</code></td>
          <td>Cost grows with two nested passes through the data</td>
          <td>Bubble sort and selection sort in time complexity</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Exam focus">
      <p>
        At IB level, Big O is used to compare scalability clearly. The important judgement is whether
        one algorithm grows more efficiently than another as the data set becomes large.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B2.4.2"
    summary="Construct and trace search algorithms by matching the method to whether the data is sorted and how much of the structure may need to be examined."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Search algorithms retrieve a target value from stored data, but they do not all place the same
      demands on the data structure. Linear search works on any sequence. Binary search is more
      efficient, but only when the data is already sorted.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-linear-search-vs-binary-search">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Algorithm</th>
          <th scope="col">How it works</th>
          <th scope="col">Requirement</th>
          <th scope="col">Worst-case time</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Linear search</td>
          <td>Checks items one by one until the target is found or the structure ends</td>
          <td>No sorting needed</td>
          <td><code>O(n)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Binary search</td>
          <td>Checks the middle item and repeatedly halves the remaining search space</td>
          <td>Data must be sorted</td>
          <td><code>O(log n)</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-trace-binary-search-on-sorted-data">Worked trace: binary search on sorted data</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Step</th>
          <th scope="col">Remaining data</th>
          <th scope="col">Middle value</th>
          <th scope="col">Decision</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>1</td>
          <td>4, 7, 9, 13, 18, 22, 30</td>
          <td>13</td>
          <td>Target 22 is larger, so keep the right half</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>2</td>
          <td>18, 22, 30</td>
          <td>22</td>
          <td>Target found</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B2.4.3"
    summary="Construct and trace bubble sort and selection sort, then evaluate both algorithms through time and space complexity."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Both algorithms sort data by repeated comparison, but they do so differently. Bubble sort swaps
      adjacent values when they are in the wrong order. Selection sort finds the next smallest value
      and places it into position.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-bubble-sort-vs-selection-sort">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Algorithm</th>
          <th scope="col">Main idea</th>
          <th scope="col">Time complexity</th>
          <th scope="col">Space complexity</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Bubble sort</td>
          <td>Repeatedly compare neighbouring items and swap them if needed</td>
          <td><code>O(n^2)</code> in the general case</td>
          <td><code>O(1)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Selection sort</td>
          <td>Select the smallest remaining item and place it in the next position</td>
          <td><code>O(n^2)</code></td>
          <td><code>O(1)</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Bubble sort may stop early if a pass makes no swaps, which can improve practical performance on
      nearly sorted data. Selection sort is easy to trace, but it still scans the unsorted part of the
      list on every pass.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B2.4.4, B2.4.5"
    summary="HL extends B2.4 with recursion by defining the base case, tracing recursive calls, and constructing simple recursive algorithms."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Recursion is a programming technique in which a function solves a problem by calling itself on
      a smaller version of the same problem. It is only valid if there is a clear stopping point.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-recursion-at-hl">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Part of a recursive solution</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Base case</td>
          <td>Stops the recursion and returns a direct answer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Recursive case</td>
          <td>Reduces the problem and calls the function again</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Call stack</td>
          <td>Stores pending function calls until they return in reverse order</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <pre><code class="language-python">def sum_to_n(n):
    if n == 1:
        return 1
    return n + sum_to_n(n - 1)</code></pre>

    <p>
      To trace this function for <code>n = 4</code>, the program expands the calls down to
      <code>sum_to_n(1)</code>, then returns upward: <code>1</code>, then <code>3</code>, then
      <code>6</code>, then <code>10</code>. That reverse return path is why the stack idea from B2.2
      matters here.
    </p>

    <TextbookCallout tone="warning" label="Critical condition">
      <p>
        A recursive algorithm without a correct base case will not terminate normally. In practice,
        that leads to a runtime failure rather than a valid result.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={b25}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="B2.5.1"
    summary="Construct file-processing code that opens files in the correct mode, reads or writes data accurately, and handles failure safely."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      File processing gives programs persistent storage beyond the current run. The syllabus focus is
      practical: choose the correct mode, process the data correctly, and make sure the program deals
      sensibly with missing files or write failures.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-core-file-operations">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Operation</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical mode or method</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Read</td>
          <td>Retrieve existing stored data</td>
          <td><code>r</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Write</td>
          <td>Create new content or replace existing content</td>
          <td><code>w</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Append</td>
          <td>Add new data to the end of an existing file</td>
          <td><code>a</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Close</td>
          <td>Release the file safely after processing</td>
          <td>Automatic with a context-managed block</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-reading-and-writing-a-report">Worked example: reading and writing a report</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-python">total = 0
count = 0

try:
    with open("marks.txt", "r") as marks_file:
        for line in marks_file:
            total = total + int(line.strip())
            count = count + 1

    if count == 0:
        average = 0
    else:
        average = total / count

    with open("report.txt", "w") as report_file:
        report_file.write("Average: " + str(average))
except FileNotFoundError:
    print("Input file not found")</code></pre>

    <p>
      This example reads each line from a file, processes the data, and writes a result to a second
      file. The exception handling prevents the program from crashing if the input file is missing.
    </p>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Scope boundary">
      <p>
        File processing is about storing and retrieving data persistently. Searching or sorting data
        after it has been read may be part of a larger program, but those algorithm choices belong in
        B2.4 rather than redefining what file processing means.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>
